solution_steps:
We use the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 957 and 1537.
\begin{align*}
\gcd(957, 1537) &= \gcd(957, 1537 - 957 \cdot 1) \\
&= \gcd(957, 580).
\end{align*}
Continuing,
\begin{align*}
\gcd(957, 580) &= \gcd(957 - 580 \cdot 1, 580) \\
&= \gcd(377, 580).
\end{align*}
Continuing,
\begin{align*}
\gcd(377, 580) &= \gcd(377, 580 - 377 \cdot 1) \\
&= \gcd(377, 203).
\end{align*}
Continuing,
\begin{align*}
\gcd(377, 203) &= \gcd(377 - 203 \cdot 1, 203) \\
&= \gcd(174, 203).
\end{align*}
Continuing,
\begin{align*}
\gcd(174, 203) &= \gcd(174, 203 - 174 \cdot 1) \\
&= \gcd(174, 29).
\end{align*}
Finally,
\begin{align*}
\gcd(174, 29) &= \gcd(174 - 29 \cdot 6, 29) \\
&= \gcd(0, 29) \\
&= \boxed{29}.
\end{align*}
The answer is: 29

