solution_steps:
To find the decimal representation of $\frac{8}{11}$, we perform long division:
$$
\begin{array}{c|cccc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & . & 7 & 2 \\
\cline{2-5}
11 & 8 & . & 0 & 0 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{-7} & & & \\
\cline{2-2}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{1} & & & \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & -9 & & \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & +9 & 9 & \\
\cline{3-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 1 & 0 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & -1 & 1 \\
\cline{4-5}
\end{array}
$$
We see that the remainder repeats after every 2 digits (the 72 in the decimal representation).
Therefore, the least number of digits in a repeating block of $\frac{8}{11}$ is $\boxed{2}$.
The answer is: 2

