solution_steps:
The inverse of a $2 \times 2$ matrix $\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix}$ is given by
\[\frac{1}{ad - bc} \begin{pmatrix} d & -b \\ -c & a \end{pmatrix}.\]In this case, $ad - bc = (6)(2) - (-4)(-3) = 0,$ so the inverse does not exist.
Therefore, the answer is the zero matrix $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}}.$The answer is: \begin{pmatrix}0&0\0&0\end{pmatrix}

